gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"b1313847877303cd905b4a0480824a363da8e3bf55305dde323ed7c2681f068f","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"Nature & Environment","secondarySubjects":["Science"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily focuses on the natural phenomena of migratory birds, encompassing their physiology and navigation as part of the broader field of nature and environmental science.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"0080864909b6f20d18e50a8fc08f4fcd6c85cd91118979a8b6d323484a8e50d4","summary":"New York Times Bestseller Finalist for the Los Angeles Times Book Prize A Library Journal Best Science and Technology Book of the Year An exhilarating exploration of the science and wonder of global bird migration. In the past two decades, our understanding of the navigational and physiological feats that enable birds to cross immense oceans, fly above the highest mountains, or remain in unbroken flight for months at a stretch has exploded. What we’ve learned of these key migrations—how billions of birds circumnavigate the globe, flying tens of thousands of miles between hemispheres on an annual basis—is nothing short of extraordinary. Bird migration entails almost unfathomable endurance, like a sparrow-sized sandpiper that will fly nonstop from Canada to Venezuela—the equivalent of running 126 consecutive marathons without food, water, or rest—avoiding dehydration by \"drinking\" moisture from its own muscles and organs, while orienting itself using the earth’s magnetic field through a form of quantum entanglement that made Einstein queasy. Crossing the Pacific Ocean in nine days of nonstop flight, as some birds do, leaves little time for sleep, but migrants can put half their brains to sleep for a few seconds at a time, alternating sides—and their reaction time actually improves.","publicationYear":2021,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google